.TH CJSON_DELETE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cjson_delete \- remove and free the DOM node at a dot-separated path
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_delete(cjson root, const char *path);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Navigates to the parent of the node that path addresses.  It then
removes the child and frees the whole subtree of that child.  The
path uses the same dot-separated syntax as cjson_get and cjson_set.

For a dictionary parent, the macro uses the leaf component as the
key to remove.  For a list parent, the leaf component must be a '#N'
token.  For example, "#2" removes index 2.

The macro splits the path at the LAST dot that is not escaped.  This
is why "a.b.c" targets the key "c" inside the node at "a.b".  When
path holds no dot, root is the parent.

The escape sequences '\e.' and '\e\e' give a literal dot or a
literal backslash in a key name.  This matches cjson_get and
cjson_set.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_retval_t.  Returns ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when root or path is NULL.  Also for an empty path
or an empty path component, which a leading dot, a trailing dot or
two dots together give.  Also when the leaf component is not valid
for the type of the parent.  Also for a malformed "#N" index.
ccol_key_not_found when the parent path is absent.  Also when the
leaf key or index has a correct form but is absent.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an internal allocation fails.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cjson_delete(doc, "users.#0.address");
cjson_delete(doc, "config.debug");
cjson_delete(doc, "items.#2");
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_get (3),
.BR cjson_set (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_remove (3),
.BR cjson_list_remove (3)
